1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,620
Hi, in this video we're going to look at credential stuffing, a form of brute force

2
00:00:10,620 --> 00:00:15,680
attack in which you attempt to log into a website using known username and password combinations

3
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:23,140
from other websites. These sets of credentials are usually collated from earlier data breaches.

4
00:00:23,140 --> 00:00:27,040
These attacks rely on the fact users often reuse the same credentials across multiple

5
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:33,040
different sites. As you're only attempting to access each account once, defence mechanisms

6
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:37,480
such as account locking are effectively powerless against this kind of attack, although you may

7
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:42,600
find that you still need to bypass any rate limiting that's in place. For ideas on this

8
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:47,840
you can check out the authentication topic on our Web Security Academy.

9
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:52,800
Before you start, you'll need a list of username and password pairs. If you're following along

10
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:57,800
using the Excessive Trust in Client-Side Controls lab from our Web Security Academy, we've

11
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:02,180
included correctly sorted word lists in this article.

12
00:01:02,180 --> 00:01:09,620
Once the lab has launched, try to log in.

13
00:01:09,620 --> 00:01:17,800
From the Proxy HTTP History tab, identify the request for submitting the login form, right-click

14
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:24,800
and send it to Burp Intruder. Go to the Intruder Positions tab and select the Pitchfork attack

15
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:33,860
type. Highlight the username and click on Add to mark it as a payload position and then do

16
00:01:33,860 --> 00:01:40,800
the same for the password. Go to the Payloads tab and under Payload

17
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:47,800
Sets, select Payload Set 1, Payload Type Simple List. Now paste in the list of usernames.

18
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:59,800
Then select Payload Set 2, again Simple List and paste in the list of passwords. Click Start Attack.

19
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:05,800
When the attack is finished, study the responses to look for any behaviour that may indicate a valid login.

20
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:12,800
Potentially differences in error messages, response times or status codes.

21
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:18,800
In this case, we can see one of the requests has received a 302 response.

22
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:25,800
If you want to investigate the contents of a response in detail, you can right-click and select

23
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:30,800
Send to Comparer Response and do the same for the original response.

24
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:43,800
On the Comparer tab, you can then select the two responses and click Words or Bytes to highlight any differences.

25
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:44,800
